WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.025
- On this episode NBA
star Mason Plumlee stops by.

00:00:05.417 --> 00:00:08.166
(hip hop music)

00:00:17.166 --> 00:00:20.000
- [Gary] You ask questions,

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.750
and I answer them.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:27.750
This is The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:32.417
Hey everybody.
This is Gary Vay-ner-chuk

00:00:32.417 --> 00:00:33.917
and this is episode 251

00:00:33.917 --> 00:00:37.583
of The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:37.583 --> 00:00:41.834
Pretty excited about this
because I'm a huge NBA fan and

00:00:41.834 --> 00:00:44.834
we have a real life
NBA player here with us.

00:00:44.834 --> 00:00:46.083
Mason Plumlee is here.

00:00:46.083 --> 00:00:50.125
I'm gonna give him a second to
tell everybody about himself but

00:00:50.125 --> 00:00:52.625
you also are
noticing no phone today.

00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:54.750
We've decided to go old
school on The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.250
I put out a tweet.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:57.000
We got some questions.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.834
Andy K, it's your
chance to finally be India.

00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:00.917
I'm happy for you.

00:01:00.917 --> 00:01:02.917
This is something you've
wanted for three or four years.

00:01:02.917 --> 00:01:05.083
I've seen the
notes back and forth.

00:01:05.083 --> 00:01:07.250
You know, DRock showed me
a text a couple years ago.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:08.708
Said something
like, "What the fuck?

00:01:08.708 --> 00:01:10.250
"Why does India
always get to do this?"

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.291
So this is a big moment
for you as well, my friend.

00:01:12.291 --> 00:01:13.250
I'm happy for you.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:14.458
- [Andy] Thank you.
- You're welcome.

00:01:14.458 --> 00:01:18.125
Alright, Mason, why don't
you tell the Vayner Nation

00:01:18.125 --> 00:01:20.208
a little bit about yourself.
- Yeah.

00:01:20.208 --> 00:01:22.125
You know,
obviously I play basketball.

00:01:22.125 --> 00:01:23.792
From Indiana originally,

00:01:23.792 --> 00:01:26.333
went to high school and
college in North Carolina.

00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:28.583
Spent my first two
years with the Brooklyn Nets.

00:01:28.583 --> 00:01:31.041
Two years, year and a half with
the Portland Trailblazers and

00:01:31.041 --> 00:01:35.166
then I was traded to
Denver February this season.

00:01:35.166 --> 00:01:38.750
So, you know, I'm going into
restricted free agency this

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.542
off-season so I'm
looking forward to that.

00:01:41.542 --> 00:01:43.917
Basketball's my
passion, it's what I do.

00:01:43.917 --> 00:01:47.917
It gets me out of
bed in the morning.

00:01:47.917 --> 00:01:50.250
I don't have to think about it.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.583
I just love it and I
wouldn't trade it for anything.

00:01:53.583 --> 00:01:56.917
So, that's my story
and that's how I'm here.

00:01:56.917 --> 00:01:58.041
- Couple things.

00:01:58.041 --> 00:02:02.458
Tell me about, so first of all,
being able to do what you love

00:02:02.458 --> 00:02:04.542
is like the greatest, right?
- Yeah.

00:02:04.542 --> 00:02:06.291
- What outside
of the basketball?

00:02:06.291 --> 00:02:08.917
When we start going into the
Mason's sphere of interests

00:02:08.917 --> 00:02:10.000
that's out of that.
- Yeah.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.458
- Entrepreneurship,
sci-fi, food.

00:02:12.458 --> 00:02:16.500
Give me some context
here and the viewers at home.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.125
- Two things that
I've really been drawn to,

00:02:18.125 --> 00:02:20.125
one being real estate.

00:02:20.125 --> 00:02:22.208
I've enjoyed different
real estate projects that

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:23.208
I've done with partners.

00:02:23.208 --> 00:02:25.917
Did a couple here in
Jersey when I lived in Jersey.

00:02:25.917 --> 00:02:29.834
And then some in Washington
state when I lived in Oregon.

00:02:29.834 --> 00:02:32.667
So real estate has
been very interesting to me.

00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:33.583
- You have like an awesome

00:02:33.583 --> 00:02:35.250
business model
figured out, right?

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.750
I play in a new
market, buy up. Right?

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:38.333
- Yeah.

00:02:38.333 --> 00:02:41.083
- You want to get traded and
do stuff in free agency just to

00:02:41.083 --> 00:02:42.583
build your real estate empire.

00:02:42.583 --> 00:02:45.458
- No, honestly, you always want
to be the player that's Kobe.

00:02:45.458 --> 00:02:48.542
One franchise your whole but if
it doesn't happen why not take

00:02:48.542 --> 00:02:50.125
advantage of being all over.
- Pretty cool.

00:02:50.125 --> 00:02:51.208
So real estate has
been interesting to you.

00:02:51.208 --> 00:02:51.875
- Real estate's
been interesting.

00:02:51.875 --> 00:02:53.000
- And fruitful. Right?
- Yeah.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:54.041
- I'm thinking about
your career and how long

00:02:54.041 --> 00:02:54.792
you've been in the league.

00:02:54.792 --> 00:02:55.750
The real estate
market has been really

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:58.125
strong during those years.
- Yeah, for sure.

00:02:58.125 --> 00:03:00.166
- So what?
You buy things and rent out?

00:03:00.166 --> 00:03:01.500
- I've done rehab projects.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.000
I've invested in other people's
projects but then also just

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.500
starting to explore development
with a partner that I have.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.917
To me it's I've always wanted to
take advantage of like I have a

00:03:13.917 --> 00:03:15.125
window where
I know I have contracts

00:03:15.125 --> 00:03:16.959
and money coming in but

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:20.959
NBA you can't
play until you're 60.

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:22.000
- You're not golfin'.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.708
- Right, so there's a finite
time to a career and I want to

00:03:25.708 --> 00:03:28.875
take advantage of the earnings
that I have during this time and

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:31.041
put aside some
assets for when I retire.

00:03:31.041 --> 00:03:32.250
- And not just earnings, right?

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:33.166
I mean here we are sitting.

00:03:33.166 --> 00:03:36.375
Obviously, we got connected
through the inner weavings of

00:03:36.375 --> 00:03:39.291
our organization but
you're obviously thinking about,

00:03:39.291 --> 00:03:42.041
look, 40 million
people email this place,

00:03:42.041 --> 00:03:43.083
"I want to be on the show."

00:03:43.083 --> 00:03:45.333
Obviously, not only the dollars
and what you can deploy but

00:03:45.333 --> 00:03:47.250
having the platform
of the NBA itself--

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.834
- For sure.
- that gives you opportunities.

00:03:48.834 --> 00:03:50.875
How are you
thinking about the leverage

00:03:50.875 --> 00:03:52.917
of just the awareness of brand?

00:03:52.917 --> 00:03:54.583
Are people willing to
say yes to things

00:03:54.583 --> 00:03:55.166
while you're in the league?

00:03:55.166 --> 00:03:56.917
I think one of the things
that I always tell players,

00:03:56.917 --> 00:03:59.917
I started a sports agency
we're starting to rep players.

00:03:59.917 --> 00:04:01.083
My brother AJ's running it.

00:04:01.083 --> 00:04:02.333
Today's a big day.

00:04:02.333 --> 00:04:05.417
We may have one of our
first players ever get drafted.

00:04:05.417 --> 00:04:08.500
I tell them, look,
I remember this vividly.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.166
I told a kid
right to his face, I go,

00:04:10.166 --> 00:04:11.583
"Look, the only
reason I'm sitting with you

00:04:11.583 --> 00:04:13.250
"is you're a New York Jet."
- Right.

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:14.917
- And the Jets are
like my heart and soul.

00:04:14.917 --> 00:04:17.500
I'm like,
"If you're a Bronco tomorrow,

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:18.708
"I'm gonna lose your number."

00:04:18.708 --> 00:04:20.583
And I actually meant it.

00:04:20.583 --> 00:04:23.041
Not like to razz him
because I was tell him

00:04:23.041 --> 00:04:24.708
he was acting like a dope.
- Yeah.

00:04:24.708 --> 00:04:27.166
- And he wasn't taking advantage
of all the things he could be

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:29.083
taking advantage of.
- Right.

00:04:29.083 --> 00:04:31.959
- You also, it's funny
you said North Carolina.

00:04:31.959 --> 00:04:34.625
You went to Duke, right?
- Yes.

00:04:34.625 --> 00:04:36.041
- (blows raspberry) It's one of

00:04:36.041 --> 00:04:38.250
the most popular
colleges in the world.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:42.542
There's just hundred and
thousands of people that are

00:04:42.542 --> 00:04:44.583
ruling the world that are
willing to meet with because you

00:04:44.583 --> 00:04:46.083
went to that university.
- Yeah.

00:04:46.083 --> 00:04:47.875
- The access is insane.
- Yeah.

00:04:47.875 --> 00:04:50.083
And I had great veterans when

00:04:50.083 --> 00:04:52.250
I came in as a
rookie in the league.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:53.583
Kevin Garnett
told me, he's like,

00:04:53.583 --> 00:04:55.333
"Look, you can
pretty much sit down or

00:04:55.333 --> 00:04:56.708
"meet whoever you want to."

00:04:56.708 --> 00:04:58.667
He said, "You just have to take
the initiative to reach out to

00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:02.250
"'em and they'll take your
call or whatever you want to do,

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:03.208
"they'll at
least listen to you."

00:05:03.208 --> 00:05:04.333
- Did he then
punch you in the face?

00:05:04.333 --> 00:05:06.375
'Cause he seems like
that kind of character.

00:05:06.375 --> 00:05:09.750
- No, yeah but he was right.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:11.625
And into that point too he also
made the point that he's like,

00:05:11.625 --> 00:05:13.208
"Once you're done
playing," he's like,

00:05:13.208 --> 00:05:14.417
"they probably
aren't picking up.

00:05:14.417 --> 00:05:16.125
"You might not get to sit down.

00:05:16.125 --> 00:05:17.708
"You might not get the
return call, whatever."

00:05:17.708 --> 00:05:20.291
But he was like,
"Don't wait until you're 40."

00:05:20.291 --> 00:05:23.417
He was playing with me when he
was 38 years old but he's like,

00:05:23.417 --> 00:05:25.375
"Don't wait 'til you're
40 to pick up the phone,

00:05:25.375 --> 00:05:28.208
"to take advantage of
the platform that you have."

00:05:28.208 --> 00:05:29.542
- You know it's
funny you just said that.

00:05:29.542 --> 00:05:32.500
One of the things that would be
interesting is to think about

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:35.333
not only the real estate
arbitrage when you're in a new

00:05:35.333 --> 00:05:37.458
markets but the
social graph, right?

00:05:37.458 --> 00:05:39.291
So like, it's kind
of interesting, right?

00:05:39.291 --> 00:05:42.458
Like who are the 5 to
25 men and women that you

00:05:42.458 --> 00:05:43.834
want to meet when
you're in Portland.

00:05:43.834 --> 00:05:46.500
Literally when you get traded
to Denver like is one of the

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.166
processes, and this is for all
the aspiring athletes out there

00:05:49.166 --> 00:05:50.500
or actual athletes.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.125
You know, okay,
now I'm in Denver.

00:05:52.125 --> 00:05:56.750
Like literally, who are the
15 to 25 business or cultural

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:59.333
leaders of Denver that I want
to meet and you pull it off.

00:05:59.333 --> 00:06:00.291
- Yeah.
- It's interesting.

00:06:00.291 --> 00:06:01.959
Okay. How was Duke?

00:06:01.959 --> 00:06:03.708
- Duke was great,
man. I enjoyed it.

00:06:03.708 --> 00:06:04.917
I stayed all four years.

00:06:04.917 --> 00:06:07.041
Guys don't do that anymore.

00:06:07.041 --> 00:06:08.083
I enjoyed it enough to stay.

00:06:08.083 --> 00:06:10.750
- You're like a unicorn.
- I know, right?

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:13.834
But no, it was a lot of
fun. Had a great experience.

00:06:13.834 --> 00:06:17.041
- Fan base is ridiculous, right?
- Fans are the best.

00:06:17.041 --> 00:06:18.834
- What about when
they're pissed at you?

00:06:18.834 --> 00:06:19.625
- Yeah, that's hard.

00:06:19.625 --> 00:06:21.542
- When they were pissed at you,
were they tough or were they

00:06:21.542 --> 00:06:23.208
kinda fully bought in
and they won so much and

00:06:23.208 --> 00:06:24.500
you guys have a such a winning.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:25.083
I always think that

00:06:25.083 --> 00:06:27.375
winning organizations
are soft, really.

00:06:27.375 --> 00:06:28.125
- Yeah.
- You know?

00:06:28.125 --> 00:06:30.583
Like they're winning often,
they could only get.

00:06:30.583 --> 00:06:34.125
When I get mad at a Jet player,
I hate them with all my heart

00:06:34.125 --> 00:06:36.291
because we haven't won ever.
- Right, right.

00:06:36.291 --> 00:06:37.959
- And so I'm like I'm really
fucking pissed at you, dude.

00:06:37.959 --> 00:06:39.083
Like I want to win.

00:06:39.083 --> 00:06:41.333
Whereas with the
Yankees when I won a bunch,

00:06:41.333 --> 00:06:42.750
I'm like yeah,
I'm mad at you but

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.250
we won so many championships.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:45.542
Like no big deal, bro.

00:06:45.542 --> 00:06:48.750
- Yeah, well, you know they had
a rough stretch before I got to

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:51.333
school and then the group I was
with my freshman year we won the

00:06:51.333 --> 00:06:53.834
championship so it was a good--

00:06:53.834 --> 00:06:55.583
- You started off
on the right foot.

00:06:55.583 --> 00:06:57.583
- And then it was like the next
three years you're trying to get

00:06:57.583 --> 00:07:00.625
back to that and it never
happened so you have to enjoy

00:07:00.625 --> 00:07:02.917
the moment and take it for what
it is 'cause it's not guaranteed

00:07:02.917 --> 00:07:03.750
to happen again.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:05.583
You go there one time in your
freshman year and you're like,

00:07:05.583 --> 00:07:07.000
"Oh, this is just what happens."

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:07.917
That's not the case.

00:07:07.917 --> 00:07:10.500
- Knowing a lot of
my fan base is a mix,

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:12.458
I think some of them may
not know a lot about you.

00:07:12.458 --> 00:07:14.875
Tell them a little bit about the
family dynamics that I think are

00:07:14.875 --> 00:07:16.083
really intriguing with you.

00:07:16.083 --> 00:07:19.083
- Yeah, so I have two brothers
who play in the NBA as well.

00:07:19.083 --> 00:07:21.250
I have a little brother with the
New York Knicks and then I have

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.333
an older brother with
the Charlotte Hornets.

00:07:23.333 --> 00:07:25.542
We all had one year
together in high school,

00:07:25.542 --> 00:07:28.834
one year together in college
but those are my best friends.

00:07:28.834 --> 00:07:31.083
I think I've alway benefitted
from watching my older brother

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:34.417
go through the same process
that I'm about to embark on.

00:07:34.417 --> 00:07:36.125
And then I've been able
to help my little brother,

00:07:36.125 --> 00:07:39.750
too so it's been a
great dynamic for me.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.417
And then also I have
a little sister who,

00:07:41.417 --> 00:07:43.500
she plays
volleyball at Notre Dame.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:45.333
And now I'm just like a
cheerleader 'cause I don't know

00:07:45.333 --> 00:07:46.375
anything about volleyball.

00:07:46.375 --> 00:07:47.750
But I'm happy for her.

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:49.500
- Dude, are your parents like

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:51.959
the greatest
athletes of all time?

00:07:51.959 --> 00:07:53.750
- No, they were athletes.

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:57.583
They weren't the greatest but
they would tell you that too.

00:07:57.583 --> 00:08:00.458
But we grew up--
- It's crazy to think--

00:08:00.458 --> 00:08:01.208
- Yeah.

00:08:01.208 --> 00:08:02.834
- like forget about
even having the DNA,

00:08:02.834 --> 00:08:04.375
the parenting.
- Mhmmm.

00:08:04.375 --> 00:08:05.583
- The serendipity.
- Yeah.

00:08:05.583 --> 00:08:07.041
- The great fortune.
- Yep.

00:08:07.041 --> 00:08:11.708
- To have three boys in
the NBA is so mind bending.

00:08:12.667 --> 00:08:14.375
- Yeah, I mean you
see a lot of brothers,

00:08:14.375 --> 00:08:15.083
a lot of twins.

00:08:15.083 --> 00:08:16.166
- [Gary] Yes.
- But,--

00:08:16.166 --> 00:08:16.708
- [Gary] Three is--

00:08:16.708 --> 00:08:19.166
- we're happy that
Marshall joined the NBA family.

00:08:19.166 --> 00:08:20.708
- [Gary] Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:08:20.708 --> 00:08:24.083
- The three thing, how
often has that happened?

00:08:24.083 --> 00:08:24.917
Three brothers in the league.

00:08:24.917 --> 00:08:26.166
- One other time.
- Which was?

00:08:26.166 --> 00:08:28.750
- I think it was the Barrys.
- Right. And their dad played.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:30.750
Right, right.
- Yeah, so.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:32.958
- That's just insane.
- It doesn't happen that often.

00:08:32.958 --> 00:08:35.166
- Do you guys think that
eventually you'll go on the road

00:08:35.166 --> 00:08:37.125
like when you're
retired and you'll be all about,

00:08:37.125 --> 00:08:38.291
there's something really there.

00:08:38.291 --> 00:08:39.291
- Yeah.
- [Gary] I'm serious.

00:08:39.291 --> 00:08:41.542
- No, we could
definitely do something fun.

00:08:41.542 --> 00:08:45.667
We've always talked about, the
best part about it is spending

00:08:45.667 --> 00:08:47.250
our off-season together
and working out together.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:48.917
So I have my
little brother here,

00:08:48.917 --> 00:08:49.875
like I said with the Knicks.

00:08:49.875 --> 00:08:52.333
My older brother
likes New York too so--

00:08:52.333 --> 00:08:54.333
- You guys are setting
up camp here this--

00:08:54.333 --> 00:08:55.625
- Yeah. Pumblee--

00:08:55.625 --> 00:08:58.542
- Who's the best player?
- Me. We all say that. So...

00:08:58.542 --> 00:09:00.000
- But what's the true answer?
- Me.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.500
- It is, right?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:04.000
I would tell you the truth.
- Yeah, I believe you.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:05.291
Alright, Andy let's do this.

00:09:05.291 --> 00:09:06.917
This is your big moment.
- [Andy] My big moment.

00:09:06.917 --> 00:09:08.291
- What's the first question?

00:09:08.291 --> 00:09:10.291
- [Andy] Jason Crocker asks,

00:09:10.291 --> 00:09:12.583
"Dude dropped 40
points on me in high school.

00:09:12.583 --> 00:09:15.583
"How early did you decide to
go all-in on basketball and

00:09:15.583 --> 00:09:18.250
"commit you life
to that pursuit?"

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:20.083
- Well, now I have a new
career high in high school.

00:09:20.083 --> 00:09:22.166
I didn't know I had 40.

00:09:22.166 --> 00:09:23.625
- Do you know what your
career high in high school is?

00:09:23.625 --> 00:09:24.792
Of course you do.

00:09:24.792 --> 00:09:26.959
- Honestly, I really don't.
- You're that good?

00:09:26.959 --> 00:09:28.166
Fuck.
(group laughter)

00:09:28.166 --> 00:09:30.208
If my career high was like 17,

00:09:30.208 --> 00:09:31.583
I'd be like, seven.
I would know everything.

00:09:31.583 --> 00:09:33.458
I'd be like, yeah, this
kid, I crossed him over.

00:09:33.458 --> 00:09:35.291
- I have to give a
shout out to Christ School.

00:09:35.291 --> 00:09:36.583
I love my high school.

00:09:36.583 --> 00:09:38.917
It was a all-boys boarding
school in North Carolina.

00:09:38.917 --> 00:09:40.458
But we had such a
good group of guys.

00:09:40.458 --> 00:09:42.917
Our first five all
went and played in college.

00:09:42.917 --> 00:09:46.166
So it was such a
fun team to be on.

00:09:46.166 --> 00:09:48.625
Our goal every year is
like, can we go undefeated.

00:09:48.625 --> 00:09:50.583
We never got it, but--

00:09:50.583 --> 00:09:51.375
- You weren't one of those guys

00:09:51.375 --> 00:09:52.417
that came up in
high school where

00:09:52.417 --> 00:09:54.417
you were the guy dropping 64?

00:09:54.417 --> 00:09:55.417
- Yeah.

00:09:55.417 --> 00:09:57.041
That's funny you asked that.

00:09:57.041 --> 00:10:00.917
But I didn't have 40
in a game, but anyway.

00:10:00.917 --> 00:10:02.208
For me, it was a
passion early on.

00:10:02.208 --> 00:10:04.500
In Indiana,
everybody plays basketball.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:06.583
- Sure, it's religion.
- Yeah, it is, it really is.

00:10:06.583 --> 00:10:08.875
Like Texas and football.
- Are you a Colts fan?

00:10:08.875 --> 00:10:10.458
- I am, yeah.

00:10:10.458 --> 00:10:12.750
Not a Pacers fan, but Colts fan.

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:14.417
But growing up--

00:10:14.417 --> 00:10:15.500
- Oh, why not a Pacers fan?

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:17.875
- You know what, when I first
started watching the NBA,

00:10:17.875 --> 00:10:20.500
it was always Utah
and Chicago in the finals,

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:21.667
so I love Chicago.

00:10:21.667 --> 00:10:23.083
I watched Jordan all the time.

00:10:23.083 --> 00:10:25.500
- So you're a
bandwagon Bulls fan?

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:26.333
- Yeah.

00:10:26.333 --> 00:10:29.041
I was northwest Indiana, though,
so I was close to Chicago.

00:10:29.041 --> 00:10:30.750
- That's how you
decided to justify that?

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:31.708
- And they were winning.

00:10:31.708 --> 00:10:33.708
- I know what they were doing.

00:10:33.708 --> 00:10:36.792
Alright, so was it
from birth, you were all in?

00:10:36.792 --> 00:10:38.125
- No, I mean, when I was young

00:10:38.125 --> 00:10:39.875
and just playing at
recess on the playground

00:10:39.875 --> 00:10:41.542
with my brothers--

00:10:41.542 --> 00:10:42.959
- Were you always
ridiculously tall?

00:10:42.959 --> 00:10:44.250
- No, I wasn't always tall.

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:45.959
And I think that's, in the NBA,

00:10:45.959 --> 00:10:47.250
not the NBA but in basketball,

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:48.542
you get the guys who are tall,

00:10:48.542 --> 00:10:50.375
so they're like,
"Well, I'll play basketball.

00:10:50.375 --> 00:10:51.166
"I'll put this to use."

00:10:51.166 --> 00:10:54.667
But it was always a passion of
mine from when I was little.

00:10:54.667 --> 00:10:56.166
It's not something
that I decided to do

00:10:56.166 --> 00:10:58.917
because I ended up being tall.

00:10:58.917 --> 00:11:00.750
- So when did you go all-in?

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:01.708
- All-in?

00:11:01.708 --> 00:11:04.417
Honestly, to me
the first, I would say,

00:11:04.417 --> 00:11:06.208
business move
concerning basketball

00:11:06.208 --> 00:11:07.917
was when I
decided to leave my home

00:11:07.917 --> 00:11:09.166
and go to a boarding school.

00:11:09.166 --> 00:11:11.417
Because up until
then, I loved it.

00:11:11.417 --> 00:11:13.583
I would just go and
play for the fun of it.

00:11:13.583 --> 00:11:15.083
- And when was that?

00:11:15.083 --> 00:11:17.208
- I did that my
sophomore year high school.

00:11:17.208 --> 00:11:19.208
- You have a big freshman year?

00:11:19.208 --> 00:11:20.708
- I didn't have a
big freshman year.

00:11:20.708 --> 00:11:24.375
I played varsity in JV
at my hometown high school.

00:11:24.375 --> 00:11:26.583
- Did you grow in
that summer like a machine?

00:11:26.583 --> 00:11:29.500
- No, I was like 6'7",
6'8" in my freshman year.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:32.625
(Gary laughs)

00:11:32.625 --> 00:11:35.250
- I love that he's like,
it's your own reality, right?

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:37.917
He just hangs out with seven
footers all day, I get it.

00:11:37.917 --> 00:11:39.166
- Yeah, but to me
I was going all-in

00:11:39.166 --> 00:11:42.250
because I didn't,
basketball was for fun.

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:46.041
And that was the first sacrifice
made toward the career.

00:11:46.041 --> 00:11:47.125
- [Gary] Let's move on.

00:11:47.125 --> 00:11:48.917
- [Andy] Ottawahoops asks,

00:11:48.917 --> 00:11:51.250
"how can student
athletes use social media

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:53.250
"to monetize their brand?"

00:11:55.417 --> 00:11:57.708
- I'll jump in on this one.

00:11:58.708 --> 00:12:02.041
I think the NCAA is

00:12:02.041 --> 00:12:04.333
ridiculously difficult
to navigate.

00:12:04.333 --> 00:12:06.208
And I have real
venom towards it.

00:12:06.208 --> 00:12:07.208
And I don't know every detail.

00:12:07.208 --> 00:12:08.291
I have real venom towards it

00:12:08.291 --> 00:12:09.750
because I also grew
up in the wine business,

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:12.625
where the laws are
ridiculous and not practical,

00:12:12.625 --> 00:12:14.125
and are archaic,

00:12:14.125 --> 00:12:15.583
and then it gets even
worse when you really look

00:12:15.583 --> 00:12:17.417
under the underbelly of
why the rules sit there.

00:12:17.417 --> 00:12:19.083
So I think student
athletes have to be careful

00:12:19.083 --> 00:12:21.333
because they can lose
that eligibility, right?

00:12:21.333 --> 00:12:23.583
Like, were you
always scared like that?

00:12:23.583 --> 00:12:25.458
It was always like
a bad cloud, right?

00:12:25.458 --> 00:12:26.917
And Coach K was
like, "Don't fuck up!"

00:12:26.917 --> 00:12:29.250
- Right, now, you don't
want to mess up his program.

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:30.458
He's not gonna like that.

00:12:30.458 --> 00:12:31.375
But that's the thing.

00:12:31.375 --> 00:12:34.583
The regulations and the rules,

00:12:34.583 --> 00:12:37.000
you shouldn't be
trying to monetize yourself

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:38.041
because they're
gonna hit you over the head

00:12:38.041 --> 00:12:39.250
with the suspension.

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:40.083
- That's why I have an answer.

00:12:40.083 --> 00:12:42.917
What your job to do,
is to really build,

00:12:42.917 --> 00:12:44.875
if you care about that, if
you're that entrepreneurial,

00:12:44.875 --> 00:12:49.041
and if you're javelin
and you're in volleyball,

00:12:49.041 --> 00:12:50.667
there's no women's
professional volleyball league

00:12:50.667 --> 00:12:51.875
that's gonna pay your sister

00:12:51.875 --> 00:12:53.375
the kind of money the boys made.

00:12:53.375 --> 00:12:55.792
So if she's thinking
about the entrepreneurial level,

00:12:55.792 --> 00:12:57.500
what you do while
you got that attention,

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:01.083
or, if you're the
11th guy at Indiana State

00:13:01.083 --> 00:13:03.500
and you don't think
you're going to the NBA,

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:04.875
what I think when you
have that attention is,

00:13:04.875 --> 00:13:06.041
you build a fan base.

00:13:06.041 --> 00:13:08.542
You don't monetize it,
you build the platform.

00:13:08.542 --> 00:13:09.750
The way to do that,

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:11.792
whether you're a
superstar player

00:13:11.792 --> 00:13:15.250
or the 11th guy on
Indiana State, is to engage.

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:17.000
Now, if you're a
superstar player,

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:18.208
and you're going to Duke,

00:13:18.208 --> 00:13:19.917
you're probably
worried about your class,

00:13:19.917 --> 00:13:21.542
you're worried
about the program,

00:13:21.542 --> 00:13:23.041
so you probably have less time.

00:13:23.041 --> 00:13:25.083
If you're 11th
guy at Indiana State,

00:13:25.083 --> 00:13:26.291
you probably have more time.

00:13:26.291 --> 00:13:28.417
And so you've
gotta just pull levers.

00:13:28.417 --> 00:13:29.959
But it's about engagement.

00:13:29.959 --> 00:13:33.834
Again, I'm hot on this because
it's a Larry Bird reference,

00:13:33.834 --> 00:13:34.875
I didn't want to use Indiana.

00:13:34.875 --> 00:13:36.291
Like it was,
I thought it was funny.

00:13:36.291 --> 00:13:39.250
So if you're
Indiana State, 11th guy,

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:41.083
every single
person that references

00:13:41.083 --> 00:13:44.250
your game on Twitter is
something you can engage with.

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:45.917
Maybe you didn't play,

00:13:45.917 --> 00:13:47.792
but it would be really
funny if Tyler was like,

00:13:47.792 --> 00:13:49.208
"You guys sucked tonight."

00:13:49.208 --> 00:13:51.375
And you jump in
and like, "Yo, bro."

00:13:51.375 --> 00:13:53.542
They'd be pumped because they
actually watched that game.

00:13:53.542 --> 00:13:55.583
That context building,
now all of sudden

00:13:55.583 --> 00:13:58.750
you leave school
with 80,000 followers

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:00.750
where you would
have had maybe 1500

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:01.917
if you didn't give a crap.

00:14:01.917 --> 00:14:04.000
And now all of a
sudden you are tweeting out,

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.166
looking for
opportunities this summer.

00:14:06.166 --> 00:14:07.333
That's where
you're at your height

00:14:07.333 --> 00:14:09.083
because you just
went through the program,

00:14:09.083 --> 00:14:10.500
and six or seven leads come in,

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:11.583
and away you go.

00:14:11.583 --> 00:14:14.917
That's really the only way
within the rules of the NCAA.

00:14:14.917 --> 00:14:16.250
Agreed, move on.

00:14:17.708 --> 00:14:18.542
- [Andy]Rodney.

00:14:18.542 --> 00:14:20.750
- Rodney, Rodney Pete?

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:21.959
- His handle's DJ Drive.

00:14:21.959 --> 00:14:23.750
- Do you know
who Rodney Pete is?

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:24.583
Just yes or no.

00:14:24.583 --> 00:14:26.500
Do you know who Rodney Pete is?

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:27.583
- [Man] No.
- Rodney Pete?

00:14:27.583 --> 00:14:28.417
- [DRock] No.

00:14:28.417 --> 00:14:32.041
- Rodney Pete, Rodney Pete,
Rodney Pete, Rodney Pete?

00:14:32.041 --> 00:14:32.917
Sorry, Rodney.

00:14:32.917 --> 00:14:34.917
USC quarterback,
went to the Lions,

00:14:34.917 --> 00:14:36.583
had a nice career.

00:14:36.583 --> 00:14:37.583
These youngsters
don't know, Rodney.

00:14:37.583 --> 00:14:40.667
- Maybe you should know
the Lions, come on Trick.

00:14:40.667 --> 00:14:42.041
- [Trick] My
family's from Michigan.

00:14:42.041 --> 00:14:43.875
- You're a big
disappointment, man.

00:14:43.875 --> 00:14:44.708
- [Trick] I've
been through all that.

00:14:44.708 --> 00:14:45.667
(Gary laughs)

00:14:45.667 --> 00:14:47.291
- Let's move on.

00:14:47.291 --> 00:14:49.041
- [Andy] Rodney asks, "What did

00:14:49.041 --> 00:14:51.583
"the NBA teach you about
basketball that you can apply

00:14:51.583 --> 00:14:54.583
"to marketing or
branding on social media?"

00:14:54.583 --> 00:14:55.542
- So, the first thing they do

00:14:55.542 --> 00:14:56.500
is they sit you
down and show you

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:58.458
what you can get fined for.
(Gary laughs)

00:14:58.458 --> 00:15:00.041
But surprisingly,
a lot of the stuff,

00:15:00.041 --> 00:15:02.125
because I was watching
what guys got in trouble for,

00:15:02.125 --> 00:15:04.542
all that stuff created such buzz

00:15:04.542 --> 00:15:07.041
and got them all (laughs)--

00:15:07.041 --> 00:15:08.667
- You're like the
arbitrage was worth it, yeah.

00:15:08.667 --> 00:15:10.834
- Too much shocked
and awe, but anyway.

00:15:10.834 --> 00:15:14.583
To me, the best advice that
they gave us was be yourself.

00:15:14.583 --> 00:15:18.375
Don't make every post
about a product pitch.

00:15:18.375 --> 00:15:21.542
Right away, as soon as
you come out of the league,

00:15:21.542 --> 00:15:24.834
or as soon as you
finish university,

00:15:24.834 --> 00:15:26.542
then you can accept money for,

00:15:26.542 --> 00:15:28.083
Then you can start--
- Sneakers.

00:15:28.083 --> 00:15:30.000
- Monetizing, right.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.083
Right away you're
gonna have people,

00:15:31.083 --> 00:15:32.917
"Hey, tweet this.
Pitch my product.

00:15:32.917 --> 00:15:33.917
"Whatever, I'll pay you."

00:15:33.917 --> 00:15:35.583
- Sports cards.

00:15:35.583 --> 00:15:36.417
- It's good and well,

00:15:36.417 --> 00:15:38.375
but you don't
want your social media

00:15:38.375 --> 00:15:39.834
to be flooded with that.

00:15:39.834 --> 00:15:42.208
You just want to be
genuine, be who you are.

00:15:42.208 --> 00:15:43.875
I think it's also good to share,

00:15:43.875 --> 00:15:47.959
don't just reply to
tweets and whatever after wins.

00:15:47.959 --> 00:15:49.667
Be the same person,
wins and losses.

00:15:49.667 --> 00:15:54.125
Don't go into a whole,
depending on the situation.

00:15:54.125 --> 00:15:55.458
- Be careful who
you're responding to and how.

00:15:55.458 --> 00:15:58.417
- Be genuine,
be authentic, yeah,

00:15:58.417 --> 00:16:01.250
always respond to real people.

00:16:01.250 --> 00:16:02.458
I think there
are a lot of people

00:16:02.458 --> 00:16:05.417
hiding behind
different accounts out there,

00:16:05.417 --> 00:16:06.708
stuff like that.

00:16:06.708 --> 00:16:08.750
- Yeah, Dunk. (laughs)

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:10.417
Kidding, I'm kidding.

00:16:10.417 --> 00:16:15.041
Talk to me about the
biggest thing you learned

00:16:15.041 --> 00:16:16.583
in your first season of the NBA.

00:16:16.583 --> 00:16:19.542
Nothing to do with marketing,
just macro learning.

00:16:19.542 --> 00:16:21.583
You know, this has
probably been on your mind

00:16:21.583 --> 00:16:24.208
as a little kid, then
definitely got more serious

00:16:24.208 --> 00:16:26.375
after your freshman
year of high school.

00:16:26.375 --> 00:16:28.625
Here it is, this is it.
You're in the league.

00:16:28.625 --> 00:16:30.375
What's the biggest
thing you learned in year one?

00:16:30.375 --> 00:16:32.750
Kevin obviously sat down and
gave you some good advice.

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:34.333
You had some
good bets on that team

00:16:34.333 --> 00:16:35.166
that you stumbled into.

00:16:35.166 --> 00:16:36.417
- Great bets, yeah.

00:16:36.417 --> 00:16:37.458
- Like macro.

00:16:39.333 --> 00:16:42.583
- To me, the biggest
thing I took away is,

00:16:42.583 --> 00:16:44.458
there's so much,

00:16:44.458 --> 00:16:46.125
I came from a college
where there was stability.

00:16:46.125 --> 00:16:48.417
Coach K was my coach every year,

00:16:48.417 --> 00:16:51.250
I had, for the most part,
similar teammates every year.

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:52.917
The NBA and, you know, I talked

00:16:52.917 --> 00:16:55.417
to my friends with other jobs,

00:16:55.417 --> 00:16:56.583
there's so much turnover,

00:16:56.583 --> 00:16:58.583
there's so much
unpredictability.

00:16:58.583 --> 00:17:00.834
Like, I went into
practice one morning

00:17:00.834 --> 00:17:02.834
and they said, "Hey,
GM wants to talk to you.

00:17:02.834 --> 00:17:03.875
"Hey, you're going to Denver."

00:17:03.875 --> 00:17:04.791
Okay, cool.

00:17:06.541 --> 00:17:08.875
You can't
control your situation.

00:17:08.875 --> 00:17:10.040
- I'm gonna do
that to DRock tomorrow.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:10.875
I'll be like, you know what?

00:17:10.875 --> 00:17:13.541
You've been traded to
the Chattanooga office.

00:17:13.541 --> 00:17:14.666
But that's crazy, right?

00:17:14.666 --> 00:17:15.959
You have to pick
up your whole life.

00:17:15.959 --> 00:17:16.916
There's a lot of great things

00:17:16.916 --> 00:17:18.750
that comes along
with professional sports,

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:20.165
and I'm sure the
veterans tell you,

00:17:20.165 --> 00:17:22.583
once your family kicks in.
- Right.

00:17:22.583 --> 00:17:25.125
- Your daughter loves
her school in Denver,

00:17:25.125 --> 00:17:26.583
and now you're in Sacramento.

00:17:26.583 --> 00:17:29.417
- Yeah, so I think
the best piece of advice

00:17:29.417 --> 00:17:30.375
that I got from those guys,

00:17:30.375 --> 00:17:32.041
and I watched it
happen on that first team,

00:17:32.041 --> 00:17:35.333
is no matter what's
happening around the game,

00:17:35.333 --> 00:17:36.500
have the same work habits,

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:39.083
have the same approach
to practice, to the games,

00:17:39.083 --> 00:17:40.750
to shoot around,
to film session.

00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:42.708
Have an approach
that you believe in,

00:17:42.708 --> 00:17:44.291
that you can stick to--
- Everywhere.

00:17:44.291 --> 00:17:46.083
- That's not gonna
change regardless of--

00:17:46.083 --> 00:17:48.000
- And have you done that?
- Yeah, I have.

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:49.667
I mean, I'm only four years in.

00:17:49.667 --> 00:17:50.834
- But you're on
three teams in four years.

00:17:50.834 --> 00:17:52.333
- Three teams in four years.

00:17:52.333 --> 00:17:54.333
Four different head coaches.

00:17:54.333 --> 00:17:56.917
I feel like I have
found a routine

00:17:56.917 --> 00:17:58.083
in something that I believe in

00:17:58.083 --> 00:18:00.667
that I can continue
to grow and get better,

00:18:00.667 --> 00:18:01.500
regardless of the situation.

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:03.750
- Was it easier to walk
into the Denver locker room

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:05.708
after experience
being the new guy

00:18:05.708 --> 00:18:07.208
in the Portland locker room?

00:18:07.208 --> 00:18:08.208
- Yeah, for sure.

00:18:08.208 --> 00:18:10.750
- Was that weird and scary?

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:12.708
- Everything's,
I would say, unpredictable.

00:18:12.708 --> 00:18:14.542
You don't know what to expect.

00:18:14.542 --> 00:18:15.708
I was telling them,
well, actually, funny,

00:18:15.708 --> 00:18:17.000
when I went into
the Denver locker room,

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:19.083
they started playing a rap song.

00:18:19.083 --> 00:18:19.917
I did a rap song,

00:18:19.917 --> 00:18:21.917
so everywhere I go
people make fun of me for it.

00:18:21.917 --> 00:18:25.417
Which is fine,
I have fun with it.

00:18:25.417 --> 00:18:28.333
You know, that
lightened the mood.

00:18:28.333 --> 00:18:32.417
And also, the NBA, you
know players on every team.

00:18:32.417 --> 00:18:34.291
- In high school, in college,
things of that nature.

00:18:34.291 --> 00:18:34.917
- Right.

00:18:34.917 --> 00:18:36.667
- Right, cool, Andy K?

00:18:36.667 --> 00:18:38.250
- [Andy] Cody Wheat asks,

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:39.834
"What do you
think people get wrong

00:18:39.834 --> 00:18:41.750
"about professional athletes?

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:44.417
"What do you think is
the biggest misconception?"

00:18:44.417 --> 00:18:46.333
- I can tell you the
thing that bothers me the most

00:18:46.333 --> 00:18:48.291
is when people talk about

00:18:48.291 --> 00:18:50.417
athletes are stupid
or dumb or whatever,

00:18:50.417 --> 00:18:53.625
and they go broke because they
don't know what they're doin'.

00:18:53.625 --> 00:18:54.792
I don't know anybody,

00:18:54.792 --> 00:18:56.708
all the players that
I know are still in the league,

00:18:56.708 --> 00:18:58.542
but people have gone broke

00:18:58.542 --> 00:19:02.041
or they've lost
what they made out of,

00:19:02.041 --> 00:19:03.792
I could see it
happenin' more from generosity

00:19:03.792 --> 00:19:05.375
- That's exactly right.
- Than from bad business moves

00:19:05.375 --> 00:19:07.041
It's not--
- I'm so glad you said this,

00:19:07.041 --> 00:19:09.083
I apologize for cutting you off.

00:19:09.083 --> 00:19:12.458
I am stunned by that variable

00:19:12.458 --> 00:19:14.166
as we just
recruit our first class.

00:19:14.166 --> 00:19:15.083
You're exactly right.

00:19:15.083 --> 00:19:16.917
It's not that
they're dumb, it's that--

00:19:16.917 --> 00:19:17.583
- They wanna do
so much for everyone.

00:19:17.583 --> 00:19:19.291
- Their inner circle,

00:19:19.291 --> 00:19:22.041
and listen,
there's a lot of cliche,

00:19:22.041 --> 00:19:24.792
lower-middle class, poor,

00:19:24.792 --> 00:19:26.291
and it's like they wanna help.

00:19:26.291 --> 00:19:27.792
They've been praying

00:19:27.792 --> 00:19:30.792
to get through the
system to this moment.

00:19:30.792 --> 00:19:33.166
Being rich you can get injured.

00:19:33.166 --> 00:19:36.792
Coming from tough places
you could get into trouble.

00:19:36.792 --> 00:19:38.583
I think that's
exactly right, my man.

00:19:38.583 --> 00:19:40.041
They're tryin' to do good,

00:19:40.041 --> 00:19:41.542
so much so, I don't
know if you've seen this,

00:19:41.542 --> 00:19:43.166
I've already seen this,

00:19:43.166 --> 00:19:44.208
some of these kids

00:19:44.208 --> 00:19:46.375
have to go through the
very difficult decision

00:19:46.375 --> 00:19:48.583
of actually
cutting off their world

00:19:48.583 --> 00:19:51.250
because literally all
that's happening is there's,

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:53.000
I saw a kid with his cell phone

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:54.375
and in 30 minutes,

00:19:54.375 --> 00:19:56.542
get asked for tens
of thousands of dollars

00:19:56.542 --> 00:19:59.875
from 11 different people.
- Right.

00:19:59.875 --> 00:20:01.792
And it happens all the time.

00:20:01.792 --> 00:20:04.625
- And it's not,--
- I'm glad you went there man.

00:20:04.625 --> 00:20:06.750
- People are so quick to say,

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:08.250
"Bad business move,
bad restaurant,

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:10.667
"bad this bad that."

00:20:10.667 --> 00:20:12.375
The most generous
people I've ever met

00:20:12.375 --> 00:20:15.625
have been teammates in
the NBA, without a doubt.

00:20:15.625 --> 00:20:16.834
- Now, there's
also Antoine Walker,

00:20:16.834 --> 00:20:18.000
who's out of his mind.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:21.000
- Yeah.
(group laughter)

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:22.375
- And by the way, on that note,

00:20:22.375 --> 00:20:24.500
I'm making that joke 'cause
I wanna give him a compliment.

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:26.375
What he's doing right now

00:20:26.375 --> 00:20:28.583
for kids that are
coming out of the league,

00:20:28.583 --> 00:20:30.834
it takes a lot of humility.

00:20:30.834 --> 00:20:32.333
Listen, I'm sure
there's economics with it,

00:20:32.333 --> 00:20:33.667
I don't know him but,

00:20:33.667 --> 00:20:34.792
I'm sure he's getting paid

00:20:34.792 --> 00:20:37.166
and it doesn't
hurt to get the money,

00:20:37.166 --> 00:20:38.458
but he's goin'
in there and sayin'

00:20:38.458 --> 00:20:40.000
"Look at me.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:40.959
"Look what happens."

00:20:40.959 --> 00:20:43.917
Man made hundreds
of millions of dollars.

00:20:43.917 --> 00:20:45.583
Oof.
Andy K?

00:20:47.792 --> 00:20:49.542
Andy felt that one.

00:20:49.542 --> 00:20:50.542
- [Andy] Sam asks,

00:20:50.542 --> 00:20:52.375
"How are you
able to stay positive

00:20:52.375 --> 00:20:54.500
"when working towards
your goals and ambitions

00:20:54.500 --> 00:20:58.959
"without seeing
improvement on a regular basis?"

00:20:58.959 --> 00:21:01.667
- To me that's one
of the hardest things.

00:21:01.667 --> 00:21:03.417
I think whether you do-

00:21:03.417 --> 00:21:05.333
- What part of your
game was toughest for you,

00:21:05.333 --> 00:21:06.917
if you said at a macro,

00:21:06.917 --> 00:21:09.333
the thing that was hardest for
you to develop in your game,

00:21:09.333 --> 00:21:11.166
that you can rewind to now,

00:21:11.166 --> 00:21:13.291
we're goin' very
macro basketball now,

00:21:13.291 --> 00:21:15.208
what would that be?

00:21:15.208 --> 00:21:16.667
- Last summer

00:21:16.667 --> 00:21:19.000
I wanted to change the
way in which I shot the ball,

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:20.291
my form and--
- Period?

00:21:20.291 --> 00:21:22.500
- Period, yeah.
- Well that's a little crazy.

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:24.917
- Yeah, well it's crazy.
- It's hardcore.

00:21:24.917 --> 00:21:26.708
- I always felt like this:

00:21:26.708 --> 00:21:30.000
Tiger Woods broke down a
swing that had won Masters,

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.417
and then he re-worked it.

00:21:31.417 --> 00:21:33.125
I wasn't breakin'
down a shot that works.

00:21:33.125 --> 00:21:34.500
(laughing)

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:36.125
I was breakin' down a
shot that didn't work.

00:21:36.125 --> 00:21:37.083
- I love it.

00:21:37.083 --> 00:21:40.708
- I wanted to change my form,--
- And?

00:21:40.708 --> 00:21:42.750
- There were a lot of
times I didn't see progress,

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:46.166
I got with the coach and we
were in the gym every day,

00:21:46.166 --> 00:21:49.667
and it was frustrating,
stuff felt awkward,

00:21:49.667 --> 00:21:52.000
you have muscle memory and
habits that are hard to break

00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:54.000
if you've been doin'
em wrong for so long.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:58.708
So that was somethin' that I
fully committed to last summer--

00:21:58.708 --> 00:22:00.000
- And?

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:01.500
- Like the question said,

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:02.959
there were a lot
of times where you--

00:22:02.959 --> 00:22:04.083
- But where are we right now?

00:22:04.083 --> 00:22:06.458
- I feel very, I'm still,--
- In it.

00:22:06.458 --> 00:22:07.959
- I'm still in it,
I'm still on that journey,

00:22:07.959 --> 00:22:10.208
I'm very happy
with where my form is.

00:22:10.208 --> 00:22:11.542
I hit more jump shots this year

00:22:11.542 --> 00:22:13.166
than I have any
year in the league,

00:22:13.166 --> 00:22:15.875
and now I feel like I have
a base and a form that I can--

00:22:15.875 --> 00:22:17.917
- Build on top of.
- continue to improve.

00:22:17.917 --> 00:22:19.208
- Andy, you should,

00:22:19.208 --> 00:22:22.583
your jump shot has
been struggling lately.

00:22:22.583 --> 00:22:24.458
Where are you
right now with your game?

00:22:24.458 --> 00:22:25.500
- I haven't been
playing that much.

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:26.583
- Me neither. Dunk?

00:22:26.583 --> 00:22:27.583
How you feelin' about yourself?

00:22:27.583 --> 00:22:28.834
You're very athletic.

00:22:28.834 --> 00:22:29.834
- [Dunk] I always
feel good about my game.

00:22:29.834 --> 00:22:31.500
- Yeah but blindly.

00:22:31.500 --> 00:22:32.917
How's the actuality of it?
- Good.

00:22:32.917 --> 00:22:33.959
- You playing well?

00:22:33.959 --> 00:22:34.792
Jake?

00:22:34.792 --> 00:22:35.625
- [Dunk] I just
haven't played in a while.

00:22:35.625 --> 00:22:36.792
- [Gary] Jake, how you doin?

00:22:36.792 --> 00:22:37.667
- No basketball.

00:22:37.667 --> 00:22:39.000
- [Gary] No basketball.
(laughter)

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:40.625
- [Dunk] I mean, if we go up
against Mase-

00:22:40.625 --> 00:22:42.250
- Yeah listen I'll
give you a preview,

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:43.417
you're gonna lose.

00:22:43.417 --> 00:22:45.500
- [Trick] Mase, you
gotta tell 'em about Yoda.

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:47.000
- Oh yeah, nah it's--

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:47.834
- Tell me.

00:22:47.834 --> 00:22:50.291
- Like I said, I found
a shooting coach, he's--

00:22:50.291 --> 00:22:52.834
- Yoda's the name of a
dude who's a shooting coach?

00:22:52.834 --> 00:22:54.375
- Tell 'em why he's
called Yoda, though.

00:22:54.375 --> 00:22:56.083
- Oh, 'cause you have to
do all sorts of weird stuff

00:22:56.083 --> 00:22:57.542
like close your
eyes and shoot backwards

00:22:57.542 --> 00:23:00.083
while you
elevate and eating Jello?

00:23:00.083 --> 00:23:02.083
- No, it's just more
he's like a bald little,

00:23:02.083 --> 00:23:04.834
you know how you get the
gray hair goin' around here.

00:23:04.834 --> 00:23:06.583
- [Trick] He has
the record for most--

00:23:06.583 --> 00:23:08.500
- Yeah, so he didn't have like,

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:09.917
there are shooting
coaches in the NBA.

00:23:09.917 --> 00:23:10.750
- Of course.

00:23:10.750 --> 00:23:11.583
- This guy had no NBA.

00:23:11.583 --> 00:23:13.166
- Right, he's an
unofficial kind of Yoda.

00:23:13.166 --> 00:23:14.917
- [Andy] He's an old,
white little Steph Curry.

00:23:14.917 --> 00:23:15.834
- But I watched him,

00:23:15.834 --> 00:23:19.458
and the guy he made like
1503 free throws in a row,

00:23:19.458 --> 00:23:20.291
so I was like,

00:23:20.291 --> 00:23:21.500
"Hey, I wanna work with you."

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:23.041
(laughing)
- You saw that on YouTube?

00:23:23.041 --> 00:23:24.458
Or you were sitting
there tying your shoes

00:23:24.458 --> 00:23:28.125
and watchin' an old white
dude hit 1503 free throws?

00:23:28.125 --> 00:23:29.333
Just curious.

00:23:29.333 --> 00:23:31.458
- He had the film of it.

00:23:31.458 --> 00:23:33.291
On the spot, if
you walk into the gym

00:23:33.291 --> 00:23:35.542
he'll rip you off 100
in a row, and I was like,

00:23:35.542 --> 00:23:37.458
"That's so incredible."
- So awesome.

00:23:37.458 --> 00:23:42.083
- And we call him Yoda 'cause
he's little and bald and wise.

00:23:42.083 --> 00:23:43.750
- I love it.
- Good dude.

00:23:43.750 --> 00:23:44.625
- Mase, I'm glad
you stopped by man.

00:23:44.625 --> 00:23:45.458
- Thanks for havin' me.

00:23:45.458 --> 00:23:47.291
- You get to ask
the question of the day.

00:23:47.291 --> 00:23:49.417
You get to ask
any question you want,

00:23:49.417 --> 00:23:52.834
macro, micro, basketball, life,
entrepreneurship, interests,

00:23:52.834 --> 00:23:55.667
anything you want,
you'll get thousands of answers,

00:23:55.667 --> 00:23:58.166
there might be
some good insight.

00:23:58.166 --> 00:23:59.125
Fire away.

00:23:59.125 --> 00:24:00.834
- Anything.
- Anything.

00:24:02.625 --> 00:24:03.458
- Man.

00:24:04.834 --> 00:24:08.708
What's the best thing to do
in New York over the summer

00:24:08.708 --> 00:24:10.250
that people don't know about?

00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:11.166
And don't tell me Hamilton,

00:24:11.166 --> 00:24:13.166
'cause you can't
get tickets to that.

00:24:13.166 --> 00:24:14.834
- Why can't you get tickets?

00:24:14.834 --> 00:24:16.000
They pay well in the NBA.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:16.834
- They do, but--

00:24:16.834 --> 00:24:18.000
- So buy some
fuckin' tickets, Mason.

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:19.333
- Alright, I'll
buy some tickets.

00:24:19.333 --> 00:24:20.166
- Okay, good.

00:24:20.166 --> 00:24:21.917
- What's the thing to do
in New York in the summer

00:24:21.917 --> 00:24:24.542
that people don't know about
that you just have to do,

00:24:24.542 --> 00:24:25.375
how 'bout that?

00:24:25.375 --> 00:24:27.583
- Love it.
My man, thanks for stoppin' by.

00:24:27.583 --> 00:24:28.750
You keep asking questions,

00:24:28.750 --> 00:24:31.375
we'll keep answering them.

00:24:31.375 --> 00:24:34.708
(hip hop music)

